<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zamboanga del Norte</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamboanga-city">Zamboanga</a> del Norte is a province on the north-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, with its capital at Dipolog. It includes the historic town of Dapitan, where the national hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-rizal">José Rizal</a> lived in exile from 1892 to 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zamboanga-del-norte.jpg" alt="Zamboanga del Norte" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patrickroque01 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zamboanga_Del_Norte_Provincial_Capitol_road_view_(Gen._Luna,_Dipolog,_Zamboanga_Del_Norte;_10-17-2023).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamboanga-city">Zamboanga</a> del Norte lies in the Zamboanga Peninsula region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and covers 7,301 square kilometres in the northern part of the peninsula. It borders Zamboanga del Sur and Zamboanga Sibugay to the south, Misamis Occidental to the northeast and the Sulu Sea to the north and west. Its irregular coastline runs some 400 kilometres from north to south. The average elevation is 243.8 metres, the highest peak being Mount Dabiak in Katipunan at 2,600 metres, and the land near the coast is plain. Rainfall is evenly distributed through the year, giving a mild climate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamboanga-city">Zamboanga</a> is the Hispanicized form of the Sinama term samboangan, &quot;mooring place&quot;, from samboang, &quot;mooring pole&quot;; it is attested in historical records from the 17th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was the predominant religion until the Spanish spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, Augustinians and Dominicans. The Jesuit missionary Francesco Palliola of Nola was active in Dapitan and died at Ponot, now the town of Jose Dalman.</p><p>Before its creation as a province, the area formed the northern portion of the historical province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamboanga-city">Zamboanga</a>. Republic Act No. 711, issued on 6 June 1952, divided Zamboanga into two provinces, one of them Zamboanga del Sur. During the martial law that began in September 1972, the human rights lawyers Jacobo Amatong and Zorro Aguilar were shot dead by two soldiers who were never caught, and the acupuncturist Leo Alto, who helped Subanon communities defend their ancestral lands, was killed by the Philippine Constabulary at Polanco on 1 August 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital and largest city is Dipolog. The province has 25 municipalities and 2 cities, and the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 1,047,455 people. Cebuano is the main language, with Chavacano, Subanon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tausug">Tausug</a>, Samal, Filipino and English also spoken, and about half the people are Roman Catholic. Dapitan is the town where the national hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-rizal">José Rizal</a> was exiled by the Spanish colonial authorities. About half the land is farmed, mainly for corn, coconut and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, and yellow fin tuna is the main catch of the commercial fishery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zamboanga del Norte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Datu Pagbuaya led many of the survivors to the north coast of Mindanao, where they founded Dapitan, in today's <b>Zamboanga del Norte</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kedatuan-of-dapitan">Kedatuan of Dapitan</a></li></ul></section>
